sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/biocomposite-toughness-from-hierarchy--gemini-4-argon--2> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Lean 4 formalization of the Gao-Ji tension-shear chain and N-level flaw insensitivity" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/biocomposite-toughness-from-hierarchy> ;
    dct:description "Formalize in Lean 4 the recursive hierarchical fracture-energy model of Gao et al. (2003) and Fratzl et al. (2006) alongside the stimulus-response composition framework already present in `ThompsonLaws/Hygromorphs.lean` (`stimulus-response-systems-category`). Define a single stagger level as a tension-shear chain of mineral platelets of aspect ratio rho, volume fraction phi, tensile strength sigma_m, and interfacial shear yield tau_i, and derive the effective fracture energy G_1 = G_0 * f(phi, rho, tau_i / sigma_m) under the pull-out condition rho < sigma_m / tau_i.\n\nExtend the single-level operator recursively to an N-level self-similar stagger hierarchy where level k serves as the reinforcing phase of level k+1, and prove in Lean 4 the exact conditions under which toughness enhancement G_N / G_0 grows exponentially with N versus saturating due to stiffness loss ((phi_eff)^N -> 0) or platelet fracture transition. Then evaluate the formalized closed-form upper bound on the empirical parameter tuples (phi, rho, N) of nacre, bone, and antler." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "A zero-`sorry` Lean 4 proof characterizing the exact regime in (phi_k, rho_k) space where N-level hierarchy compounds fracture energy, paired with an numerical evaluation showing whether real bone and nacre sit inside the proved compounding regime." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: The proved theorem shows that under realistic total mineral volume fractions (phi >= 0.45) and interface compliances, adding hierarchical levels beyond N = 2 strictly decreases specific toughness per unit stiffness unless extrinsic bridging mechanisms outside the tension-shear recurrence are invoked." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/formalization" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Define the tension-shear chain effective modulus and pull-out vs platelet-fracture regime boundary rho_crit = sigma_m / tau_i in Lean 4. 2. Prove the single-level toughness amplification theorem and the optimal aspect ratio theorem under bounded shear transfer. 3. Define the N-fold hierarchical composition operator and prove the necessary and sufficient condition on inter-level volume fractions phi_k for G_N / G_0 to be strictly increasing in N at fixed total mineral mass. 4. Plug measured (phi_k, rho_k) values for nacre (N = 2) and cortical bone (N = 5) into the proved bound to test whether theoretical compounding matches observed toughness ratios." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): tractable" ;
    skos:note "Tool: lean4" ;
    skos:note "Tool: continuum-mechanics" ;
    skos:note "Tool: computation" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch16-02> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch16-16> ;

    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0631609100> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1007/s10704-006-7156-4> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jmps.2006.07.007> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/gemini-4-argon.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/biocomposite-toughness-from-hierarchy> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Does hierarchy itself explain the toughness of biological composites?" .


  <https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0631609100> dct:title "Materials become insensitive to flaws at nanoscale: Lessons from nature" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Gao et al. 2003, Materials become insensitive to flaws at nanoscale: Lessons from nature" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1007/s10704-006-7156-4> dct:title "Application of Fracture Mechanics Concepts to Hierarchical Biomechanics of Bone and Bone-like Materials" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Fratzl et al. 2006, Application of Fracture Mechanics Concepts to Hierarchical Biomechanics of Bone and Bone-like Materials" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jmps.2006.07.007> dct:title "On the mechanics of mother-of-pearl: A key feature in the material hierarchical structure" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Barthelat et al. 2007, On the mechanics of mother-of-pearl: a key feature in the material hierarchical structure" .
}

